# Line endings, stated rather than left to each machine's git config.
#
# This project is developed on Windows and built on Linux, and the two disagree
# about what a text file looks like. Without this, a checkout on Windows rewrites
# every file's endings, and the next commit is 196 files of noise that hides the
# one line somebody actually changed.
#
# Sources are stored with LF and checked out with LF everywhere. Git for Windows
# copes with LF in C++ and Python without complaint, and a build that produces
# different bytes depending on who ran it is worse than a slightly unusual
# checkout.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only files that genuinely want CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text. The screenshots are documentation,
# so they are committed - as binaries.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.pyd binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.lib binary
*.exe binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary

# Generated files that are committed but not hand-edited. Collapsed in diffs so
# they do not drown the file that did change.
*.lock linguist-generated
